    "content": "Vanderbilt University, founded in 1873 in Nashville, TN, is a top-tier private research university known for its rigorous academics and 5–6% acceptance rate. Known as the Commodores, the school features D1 athletics and strong research programs. Tuition for 2023-24 was approximately $63,946. Academics and Admissions Acceptance Rate: Highly selective, with a 5%–5.86% acceptance rate and around 50,000 applicants. Admissions Requirements: Competitive applicants often have a 3.89+ GPA, and 75th percentile scores of 1560 SAT or 35 ACT. Student Body: Approximately 7,200 undergraduates and 6,300 graduate students. Rankings: Frequently ranked among the top 20–25 national universities, with notable programs in education, law, and engineering. Campus Life and Structure Location: An urban campus in Nashville, Tennessee, featuring 10 schools and colleges. Expansion: A new campus in San Francisco is planned to open in 2027. Mascot: Mr. Commodore. Academics: Offers over 65 majors, with a 8:1 student-faculty ratio and average class sizes of 13. Financial Aid: The \"Opportunity Vanderbilt\" program provides financial aid, focusing on grants and scholarships rather than loans. Notable AlumniVanderbilt has a vast network of distinguished alumni, including author James Patterson, Texas Governor Greg Abbott, and former U.S. Senator Fred Thompson. Athletics Conference: Southeastern Conference (SEC). Highlights: The university boasts competitive teams in basketball, baseball, and football, often appearing in March Madness tournaments.",
